---
import { TESTFLIGHT } from "../../../data/site.ts";
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  docKey="install"
  title="Installing"
  description="Install Deptheos on macOS, Windows and Linux: which file to download, first-launch warnings, updates, uninstalling, and where it keeps data."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Installing</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Deptheos is a free download for macOS, Windows and Linux. This page goes from the download to the first
      launch on each system, then covers updates and uninstalling. The iPhone and iPad app is in public beta on
      <a href={TESTFLIGHT}>TestFlight</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section id="files">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Which file to download</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="/download/">Download</a> lists the file for each system, from the
        <a href="https://github.com/ryanmitts/deptheos/releases/latest">latest release</a> page on GitHub:
        a .dmg for macOS, a setup file or a portable zip for Windows, and a .deb, .rpm or tarball for Linux
        on x86-64 or ARM64.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="requirements">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What it needs</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>macOS</strong> 27 or later, on a Mac with Apple silicon. There is no build for Intel Macs.</li>
        <li><strong>Windows</strong> 10 or later, 64-bit.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Linux</strong>, as a preview, on x86-64 or ARM64, with glibc 2.35 or newer (Ubuntu 22.04 and later, Debian 12
          and later, Fedora 36 and later, and distributions based on them) and a Vulkan driver.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Stacking runs on the graphics card: Metal on a Mac, DirectX 12 or Vulkan on Windows, Vulkan on Linux.
        Integrated graphics will do. A computer with no usable GPU can stack on the CPU instead, much more
        slowly (see <a href="/docs/troubleshooting/#gpu">Troubleshooting</a>). 16 GB of memory is recommended:
        the memory a stack needs grows with the size of the photos, not with the number of frames.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="mac">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>macOS</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><strong>Open the .dmg.</strong> A window shows Deptheos beside a shortcut to your Applications folder.</li>
        <li><strong>Drag Deptheos onto Applications</strong>, then eject the disk image.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Open Deptheos from Applications.</strong> The app is signed with a Developer ID and notarized by
          Apple, so macOS only asks the first time whether you want to open an app downloaded from the internet.
          Click <em>Open</em>.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Run it from Applications rather than from the disk image. The command line tool needs that too: once
        the app is in Applications, Deptheos ▸ Install Command Line Tool… puts the <code>deptheos</code>
        command in <code>/usr/local/bin</code> (see <a href="/docs/cli/#install">The command line</a>).
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="windows">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Windows</h2>
      <h3>The installer</h3>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <strong>Run the setup file.</strong> It installs for your user account only, into
          <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\Deptheos</code>, so it doesn't need an administrator password.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Accept the license and choose the options.</strong> <em>Add the deptheos command to PATH, for
          the command line</em> is on at first; it lets a new terminal run <code>deptheos</code>. A desktop
          shortcut is off at first.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Finish.</strong> Setup can start Deptheos for you; afterwards it is in the Start menu.</li>
      </ol>
      <h3 id="smartscreen">"Windows protected your PC"</h3>
      <p>
        The Windows installer isn't code-signed yet, so Microsoft Defender SmartScreen may stop it with
        "Windows protected your PC". Click <em>More info</em>, check that the file is the one you downloaded
        from the release page, then click <em>Run anyway</em>. You may see the same warning when you first run
        Deptheos from the portable zip.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <h3>The portable zip</h3>
      <p>
        Extract the zip and run <code>deptheos-gui.exe</code> in the Deptheos folder it holds;
        <code>deptheos.exe</code>, beside it, is the command line. Nothing is installed, and there is no
        uninstaller: delete the folder to remove it. Its renders, settings and logs go in the same folders as
        an installed copy's (<a href="#data">below</a>).
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="linux">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Linux</h2>
      <p>
        Linux builds are a preview: they are tested on fewer systems than macOS and Windows. Each package comes for x86-64 and for ARM64 (<code>amd64</code> or <code>x86_64</code>, and
        <code>arm64</code> or <code>aarch64</code>, in the file names). The .deb and .rpm install the app as
        <code>/usr/bin/deptheos-gui</code> and the command line as <code>/usr/bin/deptheos</code>, with a menu
        entry, and pull in the libraries they need. In the folder you downloaded to:
      </p>
      <p class="cmd"><code>sudo apt install ./deptheos_*_amd64.deb</code></p>
      <p class="cmd"><code>sudo dnf install ./deptheos-*.x86_64.rpm</code></p>
      <p>
        The tarball is for other distributions, or for installing without root. Unpack it, then run its
        <code>install.sh</code>, which copies the app and the command into <code>~/.local</code> and adds the
        menu entry:
      </p>
      <p class="cmd"><code>tar xzf Deptheos-*-linux-x86_64.tar.gz</code></p>
      <p class="cmd"><code>cd Deptheos-*-linux-x86_64 &amp;&amp; ./install.sh</code></p>
      <p>
        <code>sudo PREFIX=/usr/local ./install.sh</code> installs it for every user instead. You can also run
        <code>bin/deptheos-gui</code> straight from the unpacked folder. With the tarball, install the libraries
        yourself; its <code>README.txt</code> lists them for Ubuntu, Debian and Fedora. Then open Deptheos
        from the menu, or run <code>deptheos-gui</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most desktop Linux systems already have Vulkan and desktop file dialogs set up. If Deptheos can't
        find your GPU or stacks slowly in a virtual machine, see
        <a href="/docs/troubleshooting/#gpu">Troubleshooting</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="first-launch">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The first launch</h2>
      <p>
        Deptheos opens on the Library, empty. <a href="/docs/getting-started/">Your first stack</a> goes from
        there to a saved picture. If it can't start its GPU engine, it says so and offers Settings, where you
        can choose another GPU or the CPU (<a href="/docs/troubleshooting/#gpu">Troubleshooting</a>).
      </p>
      <p>
        For camera RAW files, Deptheos has a decoder built in. On macOS and Windows it can also use Adobe DNG
        Converter, a free program from Adobe that you install separately, to get the same color as Lightroom
        gives the same files. It is optional: <a href="/docs/raw/#which">RAW, DNG and color</a> explains when
        it helps.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="updates">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Updates</h2>
      <p>
        Each time it starts, Deptheos asks GitHub whether a newer version has been released. When one has, a
        notice says "Update available" with the new version and yours, and its <em>Download</em> button opens
        that release's page in your browser. Deptheos doesn't download or install anything itself. At startup
        it stays quiet when there is no update or the check fails; to check by hand, and hear either way,
        choose Deptheos ▸ Check for Updates… on a Mac, or ≡ ▸ Help ▸ Check for Updates… on Windows and Linux. To stop the check at
        startup, turn off Settings ▸ General ▸ <em>Check for updates</em>.
      </p>
      <p>To install the new version, download its file and install it as before:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>macOS</strong>: drag the new Deptheos onto Applications and choose to replace the old one. An installed command line tool follows the app.</li>
        <li><strong>Windows</strong>: run the new setup file. It installs over the old version, closing Deptheos first if it is running and opening it again afterwards. For the portable zip, replace the folder.</li>
        <li><strong>Linux</strong>: the same <code>apt install</code> or <code>dnf install</code> command with the new file upgrades the package; for the tarball, run the new folder's <code>install.sh</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Your renders and settings are kept.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="uninstall">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Uninstalling</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>macOS</strong>: quit Deptheos and move it from Applications to the Trash. If you installed the
          command line tool, also remove its link: <code>sudo rm /usr/local/bin/deptheos</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Windows</strong>: in Windows Settings ▸ Apps, find Deptheos and choose <em>Uninstall</em>. It also takes
          the <code>deptheos</code> command off your PATH. For the portable zip, delete its folder.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Linux</strong>: <code>sudo apt remove deptheos</code> or <code>sudo dnf remove deptheos</code>.
          For the tarball, run <code>./install.sh --uninstall</code> from its folder, with the same
          <code>PREFIX</code> you installed with.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="data">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Where it keeps its data</h2>
      <p>
        Uninstalling removes the app and leaves your data. Your frames are only ever read, so nothing of yours
        is kept beside them; everything Deptheos writes is in these folders, and deleting them removes every
        render, setting and log. Export the renders you want to keep first.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>macOS</strong>: renders and settings in <code>~/Library/Application Support/Deptheos</code>,
          logs in <code>~/Library/Logs/Deptheos</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Windows</strong>: renders in <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\Deptheos</code>, with the logs in its
          <code>logs</code> folder; settings in <code>%APPDATA%\Deptheos</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Linux</strong>: renders in <code>~/.local/share/Deptheos</code>, settings in
          <code>~/.config/Deptheos</code>, logs in <code>~/.local/state/Deptheos</code> (or under
          <code>$XDG_DATA_HOME</code>, <code>$XDG_CONFIG_HOME</code> and <code>$XDG_STATE_HOME</code> when those
          are set).
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If you chose another folder under Settings ▸ Library ▸ <em>Library folder</em>, the renders are there
        instead. <a href="/docs/concepts/#files">Stacks, renders and the Library</a> explains what a render
        keeps.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
